# Put the first curated catalog in place of the empty catalog and harvester
# databases, then clear core's references to what is gone
# (k8s/catalog-import/README.md, k8s plan 0012). Core is dumped too, because the
# cleanup at the end writes to it.
#
# THIS TASK IS ARMED FOR STAGING AND FOR PRODUCTION. A staging deploy until the
# first date below runs it. Both dumps are in the bucket `velista-staging`, and
# the dry run of 2026-10-07 on the staging VPS passed every step but the one
# loss that expected-losses.txt now accepts.
#
# In production, release 0.13.2 runs it, and only until the second date below.
# The owner armed it on 2026-10-07. No other version runs it there. A production
# deploy after that date records the task as `expired`, which is final. The
# restore downloads both dumps from the bucket `velista`, and it refuses a file
# that is absent or whose SHA-256 is not the one of the manifest. A window is at
# most 14 days after the commit that writes it.
RUN_UNTIL_STAGING="2026-10-09"
RUN_UNTIL_PRODUCTION="2026-10-14"
PRODUCTION_RELEASE="0.13.2"
DATABASES="catalog harvester core"
#
# The most rows this restore may replace. check.sh and the script count the rows
# of `items` in catalog and of `source_catalog_entries` in harvester, and refuse
# above these numbers. A number above 0 is the owner's statement that the
# cluster holds that many rows which are to be replaced. Production's harvester
# held 18,537 unresolved rows on 2026-10-03, from the harvests of late September.
MAX_ITEMS_REPLACED_STAGING="0"
MAX_SOURCE_ENTRIES_REPLACED_STAGING="0"
MAX_ITEMS_REPLACED_PRODUCTION="0"
MAX_SOURCE_ENTRIES_REPLACED_PRODUCTION="18537"
